
# Assignment 2 - Environment Variable Mystery

## Scenario
The company wants every employee to have an environment variable COMPANY=ITI available automatically in every new terminal or shell session.

## Requirement
- Create the environment variable.
- Verify it exists in the current shell, a child shell, and a new terminal.
- Make the variable persistent across sessions.

## Steps Taken
1. Created the variable temporarily with: export COMPANY=ITI
2. Verified it in the current shell with: echo $COMPANY
3. Opened a child shell with bash and confirmed the variable was inherited (echo $COMPANY still showed ITI), because export makes a variable available to all child processes of the current shell.
4. Opened a brand new terminal and found the variable was NOT there, because a temporary export only lives in the shell session it was run in and is lost once that session ends.
5. Made the variable persistent by adding the line export COMPANY=ITI to the end of ~/.bashrc.
6. Applied the change to the current shell without restarting it using: source ~/.bashrc
7. Opened a new terminal and confirmed COMPANY=ITI now appears automatically.

## Why ~/.bashrc Was Chosen
~/.bashrc is read automatically every time a new interactive non-login bash shell starts (such as when opening a new terminal window). Since the requirement was for the variable to be available "every time a new terminal or shell is opened," .bashrc is the correct startup file, as opposed to files like ~/.bash_profile or ~/.profile which only run for login shells.

## Deliverables
- Screenshot of a new terminal showing echo $COMPANY output as ITI.
- This report explaining the solution.
